Ladybird scales the stroke-dasharray and stroke-dashoffset of a geometry
element by its path’s length over its pathLength, for painting and for
hit testing the stroke. Blink does the same, in its SVGLayoutSupport::
ApplyStrokeStyleToStrokeData(), with the scale from SVGGeometryElement::
PathLengthScaleFactor(). WebKit does it in SVGRenderSupport::
applyStrokeStyleToContext(), and Gecko in GetStrokeDashData(), with
the scale from SVGGeometryElement::GetPathLengthScale().

All three scale a percentage dash after resolving it, though the spec
says pathLength has no effect on percentages, and so does Ladybird.
For a pathLength of zero, WebKit and Gecko turn dashing off, so the
stroke draws solid; Blink caps the scale at the largest float, which
draws a dash array without a zero solid too. Ladybird draws it solid.
All three ignore a negative pathLength. For one that doesn’t parse,
they split: Blink and WebKit treat it as zero, while Gecko ignores it,
as the spec’s error handling has it. Ladybird ignores it too, as its
own isPointInStroke() already did.
